Here's a simple example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling using the HttpClient class. This example demonstrates how to make authenticated requests to a REST API using an access token.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class RestServiceWrapper
{
    private readonly string _baseAddress;
    private readonly string _accessToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseAddress, string accessToken)
    {
        _baseAddress = baseAddress;
        _accessToken = accessToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync<T>(string resource)
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _accessToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync($"{_baseAddress}/{resource}");
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}

public class MyRestService
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var baseAddress = "https://example.com/api";
        var accessToken = "your-access-token-here";

        var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper(baseAddress, accessToken);

        // Example usage:
        var result = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync<dynamic>("users");
        Console.WriteLine(result);
    }
}
```

Replace `"your-access-token-here"` with your actual access token. This example demonstrates how to make GET requests, but you can easily adapt it to handle other HTTP methods like POST, PUT, and DELETE.

