Here's a simple example of how you can create a toast notification using React context:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';

const ToastContext = React.createContext();

function ToastProvider({ children }) {
  const [toast, setToast] = useState(null);

  function showToast(message) {
    setToast({ message, visible: true });
  }

  function hideToast() {
    setToast({ ...toast, visible: false });
  }

  return (
    <ToastContext.Provider value={{ showToast, hideToast }}>
      {children}
    </ToastContext.Provider>
  );
}

function ToastConsumer() {
  const { showToast, hideToast } = React.useContext(ToastContext);

  function handleClick() {
    showToast('Hello, world!');
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Show Toast</button>
      {toast && (
        <div className="toast">
          {toast.message}
          <button onClick={hideToast}>Close</button>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

ReactDOM.render(
  <ToastProvider>
    <ToastConsumer />
  </ToastProvider>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```

In this example, we have a `ToastProvider` component that creates a context object and provides the `showToast` and `hideToast` functions as values. The `ToastConsumer` component consumes the context object and renders a button that triggers the showing of a toast notification. When the toast is shown, it is rendered above the button. The toast can be closed by clicking on the "Close" button.

