To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can introduce a simple distance check before adding a new planet to the array. If the distance between the new planet and all existing planets is less than the desired minimum distance, you can reject the new planet and generate a new one until it meets the criteria.

Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function with this check:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to pass the desired minimum distance as an argument when calling this function in your `useEffect` hook. For example:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  // ...
  const generateRandomPlanetsWithMinimumDistance = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
    // ...
  };

  setPlanets(generateRandomPlanetsWithMinimumDistance(5, 35));
}, []);
```

This way, your planets will be distributed more evenly in the 3D space, preventing them from being too close together.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows. This utility will check for any words that are broken across lines and join them together.

```javascript
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [formattedText, setFormattedText] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    const formatText = () => {
      const words = text.split(' ');
      const formattedWords = [];

      for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        const word = words[i];
        const lastChar = word.charAt(word.length - 1);

        if (lastChar === ' ' || lastChar === '\n') {
          formattedWords.push(word);
        } else {
          const nextWord = words[i + 1];
          const nextLastChar = nextWord.charAt(0);

          if (nextLastChar === ' ' || nextLastChar === '\n') {
            formattedWords.push(word + ' ');
          } else {
            formattedWords.push(word);
          }
        }
      }

      setFormattedText(formattedWords.join(' '));
    };

    formatText();
  }, [text]);

  return <div>{formattedText}</div>;
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this utility, simply wrap your text with the `RemoveWidows` component:

```javascript
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit." />
```

This will render the formatted text without any widows.

